A||||12||Phantom Rage||88 Front lowering spring install.||||5||
Z||000000||Phantom Rage||06-19-2000||07:56 PM||||My 88 Lowering springs just came in(Fiero Store's.)  I could not find any info on removal/install of the front springs, through Haynes or Chiltons. <P>Any advice would be helpfull, Thank You!  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||pherder||06-19-2000||09:24 PM||||Take a look at the General topics ... This weekends work ...  I put The Fiero Store's lowering springs in the front.  <P>I have RCC adjustable perch struts in the rear.<P>It looks like it lowered my car by 2"!!!  More then I really wanted.<P>Is your springs red?  Mine are.  Kinda matches my red car.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Getting the stock springs out isn't too much of a problem.  Getting the shorter new springs in isn't too much of a problem.  Getting stock springs back in is a BIG problem!!!  Been there, Done that, am wearing a Fiero t-shirt.<P>The best $$ spend was for Rodney Dickman's spindle removal tool.  It is nothing more than a large nut and bolt that is machine to fit over the two ends of the upper and lower ball joint.  You loosen (but do NOT remove) the two ball joint nuts and then put this tool between them and keep screwing it out until BOTH ball joints pop out of the spindle.  The popping out is the reason you DO NOT remove the nuts, just loosen them.  Support the lower arm with a jack and then remove the nuts on the spindle, remove the spindle and lower the lower arm.  Remove the spring.  Of course you have to remove the shock BEFORE you take off the spindle and it is a good idea to remove and position the caliper some where out of the way without disconnecting it (I disconnected it to replace the brake lines with Earls braided steel lines.)<P>Paul<P><BR>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 06-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||06-19-2000||09:27 PM||||Get your car up a couple notches on the jack stands. The stock (WS6) coils are 12" long fully extended.<BR>I used a pickle fork to separate the knuckle from the ball joint. You may want to just whack the knuckle with a hammer to save the  BJ's boot.<BR>Use a hydraulic jack to lower the outboard end down in a controlled fashion (slowly!). <BR>Hopefully the new springs are shorter than the stockers, so you may be able to install the springs in the lower control arm pocket, then just jack the BJ stud up to the knuckle while pivoting on the (loosened) inner bushings.<BR>If the spring is too long (like stockers), you may have to place the spring in the completely unbolted lower arm, and jack the spring and arm up till the inner pivots line up with the tabs in the frame. Then wiggle the bolts in, then jack it up the rest of the way so the stud in the ball joint goes in the knuckle. Even with a hydraulic jack, this is a pain in the a$$. Some sort of spring compressor would be helpfull here. <BR>Be very carefull and patient when dealing with springs. If one gets loose, it'll definitely take the buttons off your shirt if it hits you!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phantom Rage||06-20-2000||09:43 PM||||Wow, thanks for the detailed info guys. Hell, who needs Haynes or Chiltons? Hehe<BR>I'll give it a try this weekend, still waiting for the rear kyb struts to come in.<P>Yes, Pherder my front springs are also red.  I feel like such an idiot though, I got (FS) rear 88 lowering springs too.  It struck me that if I could use 84-87 rear struts for my 88, then 84-87 rear lowering springs should also work.  I could have gotten suspension techniques through JC-Whitless for a little less.  I’m not really mad though since the (FS) has always treated me well <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks again guys <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000004||pherder||06-20-2000||09:59 PM||||Phantom Rage!<P>Good thing you didn't get just the springs for an 84-87 Fiero, they would NOT fit the 88!! <P>The mounting point on top ("top hat") is different and the springs are wound differently and small diameter.  The earlier springs are wider and would not have fit up inside of the strut tower.<P>The struts are the same ... the stuff that mounts to them is different!<P>You went the right way even if you didn't know!<P>BTW, I put a set of RCC adjustable perch struts on the rear so that I can adjust the rear to the height needed to make the rear look right no matter what the height of the front end is.<P>Paul||||reg||4
Z||000005||Phantom Rage||06-22-2000||09:12 PM||||Thanks for setting me straight Pherder, I would hate it if any unknowing member took my advice.  I didn't even think about the top hats.  ||||reg||7
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-23-2000||10:28 AM||||What are these RCC adjustable perch struts, what do they cost, where did you get them, and will they work for a pre-'88 car???||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||06-23-2000||04:03 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>They are the exact same thing that Batboy put on his Batmobile!  Some people call them coil-over struts but the Fiero has "coils" over their struts already!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The only difference is I got real light springs (for my Quad 4 engined car which is 30+ pounds lighter then the 2.5L it replaced).<P>My springs were 240 # but RCC (and others) can replace those springs with a much higher rate (300 # on up!)  The 240 # is about stock rate.<P>The current price with Griable Gas Struts is $395.  You can get the kit (just the adjustable perch pieces from Ryane Motorsports for around $280 (?) or from Ground Control for $199.  Both these kits require that the end user cut the stock spring perch off the strut that they have and assemble the kit on to that strut.  The RCC product has the work already done for you and is assembled before shipping.  I found a couple of years ago that it is easier for me to buy the RCC kit then to take a cut off wheel and CAREFULLY cut off the spring perch.  If you are NOT CAREFULL you can cut into the strut and ruin it!!<P>I believe the first time I used one of these coil over kits (adjustable perch kits) was from RMS since RCC didn't have theirs ready yet.  I paid $200 for the kit (back in 1994/5 (?) and spend several days cutting off that !@#$!@#$ spring perch.<P>Since all these kits use the 2.5" diameter springs, they will fit all years of the Fiero (but the 88 will have to have a section ground out so that the upright of the rear spindle will have clearance for alignment (the same thing that has to be done to the KYB struts for 88 clearance.)<P>On the older model Fieros, there isn't a problem.<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||batboy||06-23-2000||05:04 PM||||Mr. Rage, back to replacing the front coil springs, since you have to drop the lower control arm down off the lower ball joint anyway, I'd go ahead and replace that lower ball joint since it's a bear to remove it without damaging it anyway, plus the lower ball joint usually wears out faster than the upper ones. If you ever intend to install poly bushings, this is a good time to do that too.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phantom Rage||06-23-2000||07:51 PM||||When I first bought my yellow banana <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, I had to get the ball joints replaced (professionally), hopefully they are still good.  Now I'm afraid to screw up the lower ball joints, Yikes!!!  Perhaps the tool Pherder referred too might be worth while.  I was just gonna try it Gixxer's way, that is they way I have see some mechanics do it also.  Hummmm, Poly kit, what a fine Idea.<BR>                   As always, many thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||06-23-2000||09:54 PM||||The press is the only real way to minimize the risk of damage. A pickle fork will usually tear the boot at the very least and hammering it out usually causes stud damage, even if you leave the nut part way on. Never beat on a ball joint if you intend on reusing it. That can weaken the metal or cause stress fractures that might break completely later on, like at 75 MPH. Hello ditch...mayday, mayday...||||reg||1
Z||000011||gixxer||06-23-2000||10:53 PM||||When I took my front end apart with the pickle fork, my main intent was to replace the worn ball joints. The pickle fork destroyed my old ball joint boots! <BR>Sorry if I mislead you.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||06-24-2000||11:13 AM||||I always try to just give the area of the spindle where the stud goes a good whack.<BR>Someone told me about this,and it works most of the time,you just have to hit it real hard to create a shock wave that pops the stud out of the taper.You strike sideways,basically,with the hammer,not up or down,and you don't touch the balljoint.||||reg||1
